# A uniform profile of ONE candidate for ONE office, so every candidate on a ballot comes
# out in the same shape. Fill {{candidate}} and {{office}} per run. The gather angles bake
# in the accuracy checklist (search the FEC by name; read an authoritative bio, not just the
# campaign site; verify an absence before asserting it) so consistency and accuracy hold
# together. See docs/plans/REPORT_PRESET_PLAN.md.
name: candidate_profile
description: Uniform profile of one candidate for one office — bio, experience, finance, policy, endorsements, record.
output_kind: report
sources: web

question: "Candidate profile — {{candidate}} for {{office}}"

objective: |
  Produce a uniform candidate profile of {{candidate}}, running for {{office}}.
  Follow the section outline exactly and in order, using each heading verbatim as a Markdown
  heading — do not add, drop, rename, merge, or reorder the sections. Fill each section from
  the gathered findings with the facts named below:
    - Summary: two or three sentences — who they are and their standing in this race.
    - Background & Biography: birthplace, education (name each degree and institution),
      career, family, and military service if any.
    - Political Experience: offices held (with years) and prior campaigns and their results.
    - Campaign Finance: the FEC committee name and ID, total receipts, cash on hand, and
      notable donors or self-funding — as reported by the FEC, cited.
    - Policy Positions: their stance on the economy, healthcare, immigration, education, and
      energy.
    - Endorsements & Support: notable endorsements and organized backing.
    - Controversies & Record: any legal or regulatory history, notable controversies, or
      record relevant to the office — reported factually and cited.
    - Assessment: a brief, sourced read of their standing and prospects in this race.
  When a section's fact genuinely cannot be found in the gathered sources, KEEP the heading
  and write the single line "Not established — no supporting source found as of the run
  date." Never drop the section, never pad it, and never assert an absence ("has no
  committee", "no degree") the findings did not actually verify against the authoritative
  source.

sections:
  - "Summary"
  - "Background & Biography"
  - "Political Experience"
  - "Campaign Finance"
  - "Policy Positions"
  - "Endorsements & Support"
  - "Controversies & Record"
  - "Assessment"

angles:
  - title: "Biography & record"
    brief: >-
      Research the full biography and record of {{candidate}}, candidate for {{office}}.
      Read an AUTHORITATIVE profile — Ballotpedia and the official/campaign bio — not only a
      single campaign "about" page, which is often thin. Cover birthplace, education (every
      degree and the institution), career, family, and any military service, plus offices
      held and prior campaigns with results. If a detail isn't on the first page you read,
      check the reference profile before concluding it's unavailable.
  - title: "Campaign finance (FEC)"
    brief: >-
      Find {{candidate}}'s U.S. federal campaign-finance filings for {{office}} by searching
      the FEC website (fec.gov) for the candidate BY NAME and state. Report the principal
      committee name and ID, total receipts, cash on hand, and notable donors or candidate
      self-funding, as the FEC reports them. A committee is often filed under the candidate's
      own name (e.g. a "NAME FOR SENATE" / "NAME 4 SENATE") — do not conclude there is no
      committee until you have actually searched the FEC; if none appears, say "no FEC
      committee found under this name as of <date>" rather than asserting none exists.
  - title: "Policy positions"
    brief: >-
      Research {{candidate}}'s policy positions for {{office}} across the economy, healthcare,
      immigration, education, and energy. Draw from their campaign platform and any voter
      guides or interviews; where a position is unstated, say so rather than inferring one.
  - title: "Endorsements & controversies"
    brief: >-
      Research endorsements and organized support for {{candidate}} ({{office}}), and
      SEPARATELY any controversies, legal or regulatory history, or notable record. Check
      news coverage and public records, not just the campaign's own materials; report
      anything found factually and with sources, and note plainly if none is found.
  - title: "Race context"
    brief: >-
      Research the {{office}} race itself: the seat, the primary and general election dates,
      the field of candidates, and where {{candidate}} stands relative to the others (name
      recognition, resources, front-runner status). This frames the profile's assessment.
